---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Subscribe from "../components/Subscribe.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`How ${SITE.title} is produced: sources, scoring, AI assistance and the sources policy.`}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About this site</h1>
    <p>
      <strong>{SITE.title}</strong> — {SITE.tagline} One short, sourced article a day
      about whatever the tech and AI world is actually talking about.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is automated end to end, and it is deliberately conservative about
      what it claims.
    </p>

    <h2>How an article gets made</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Collect.</strong> Every morning it pulls recent items from Hacker News, arXiv,
        GitHub and Reddit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cluster.</strong> Items are embedded and grouped by meaning, so four
        sources talking about the same thing become one topic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Score.</strong> Topics are ranked by velocity (how much faster it is
        moving than usual), novelty, how many items it has, how many independent
        sources back it, and engagement normalised within each source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Draft.</strong> A language model writes a 400–600 word article from
        those sources only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Validate.</strong> The draft is rejected unless it meets length limits,
        cites only the supplied URLs, avoids placeholder text, and carries slug-safe
        tags. A rejected draft gets one rewrite; if it still fails, the day is skipped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Publish.</strong> The Sources section is generated by code — never by
        the model — and the article is marked AI-assisted. Quiet days are skipped
        rather than padded.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Sources policy</h2>
    <p>
      Every article ends with a numbered list of the exact items it was written from,
      with links. If a fact is not in those sources, the model is instructed not to
      state it — no invented numbers, dates, quotes, benchmarks or versions. The
      validator additionally rejects any URL in the body that was not in the supplied
      sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is still machine-written text: treat it as a starting point and check the
      original sources before relying on anything.
    </p>

    <h2>AI disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      Every post is marked <code>ai_assisted: true</code> in its metadata, shows an
      AI-assistance note at the end, and includes an
      <code>Article</code> entry in its structured data listing its citations.
    </p>

    <h2>What it won't do</h2>
    <p>
      It does not publish on thin days, it does not repeat a topic inside its
      cooldown window, and it does not post the same article twice — publishing is
      idempotent per target.
    </p>
  </article>

  <Subscribe />
</BaseLayout>
